paration near the temperature at which the shear area reaches 100%, connecting fracture appearance and toughness evaluation [Data: Entities (768, 538); Relationships (1003)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Tanaka equation (14) integrates composition, microstructure, and separation effects\",\"explanation\":\"Tanaka equation (14) is presented as a model for transition temperature. Its terms include chemical-composition dependence through ΔT, strengthening terms σ_ph and σ_dh, grain-size and effective-grain-size terms, subgrain volume fraction, and the number of separations [Data: Entities (821); Relationships (1070, 1071, 1072, 1085, 1086)].\\n\\nThe Tanaka model therefore provides the analytical framework connecting the community’s processing and material variables. The supplied records identify the included variables and their conceptual roles, but do not provide fitted coefficients, validation results, or numerical predictions for specific materials [Data: Entities (818, 821, 814, 827, 828)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Chemical composition affects separation initiation and model behavior\",\"explanation\":\"Chemical composition determines the constant ΔT in Tanaka equation (14), making it an explicit input to the transition-temperature model [Data: Entities (722, 821); Relationships (1071)].\\n\\nThe evidence also states that the mechanism initiating separation may depend on chemical composition. In combination with appropriate rolling conditions, composition can enable production of dual-phase steel in the as-hot-rolled condition [Data: Relationships (950); Entities (722)]. The records do not identify particular alloying elements or compositions, so no element-specific conclusions can be drawn.\"},{\"summary\":\"Charpy impact properties provide the relevant evaluation context\",\"explanation\":\"Charpy impact properties are evaluated in connection with deformation in the austenite–ferrite region, and the discussion relates them to absorbed energy and toughness [Data: Entities (550); R